+### Syntax highlighting (tree-sitter)
+
+Syntax highlighting is provided by tree-sitter and is entirely optional: both
+the runtime and the per-language parsers are loaded at runtime with
+`dlopen()`, so the editor always builds and simply shows plain text when a
+parser is missing. To enable it, install the `tree-sitter` runtime and the
+parser libraries for the languages you use (rust, python, C, C++, javascript,
+java are supported by default):
+
+- **macOS (Homebrew)**: `brew install tree-sitter tree-sitter-python` (and any
+  other `tree-sitter-<lang>` formula that exists).
+- **Linux**: install `libtree-sitter` and build the grammars with the
+  tree-sitter CLI (`tree-sitter generate`), or use your distro's packages;
+  the editor looks for `lib<lang>-tree-sitter.{dylib,so}` and
+  `libtree-sitter-<lang>.{dylib,so}` in the loader path,
+  `/opt/homebrew/lib`, `/usr/local/lib` and `~/.local/lib`.
+
+The file's extension picks the language (`.rs`, `.py`, `.c`/`.h`, `.cpp`/
+`.cc`/`.hpp`, `.js`/`.mjs`/`.jsx`, `.java`). New languages can be added as a
+small follow-up patch to the `lang_tab` table in `highlight.c`.
+
 ### Counts & operators
 
 Motions and editing commands take a leading count, like vim: `10e`, `18w`,
@@ -148,7 +169,6 @@ extend the selection (`2j`, `10l`). `Esc` cancels a pending count or operator.
 ## Explicitly out of scope (for now)
 
 - LSP / DAP
-- tree-sitter / real syntax highlighting (maybe simple regex highlighting later)
 - A runtime plugin system or embedded scripting language
 - Multiple cursors
 
